# PaperLab config bootstrap

Applies to every PaperLab subagent (`acquirer`, `dissector`, `implementer`, `explainer`, `critic`, and any future agent). Loaded automatically in every chat in this workspace.

## Resolve paths via `tools/paths.py`

Path conventions are encoded in `tools/paths.py` at the repo root. The agent MUST NOT hard-code paths or concatenate strings to build them. Use the helpers in `tools.paths`:

```python
from tools.paths import (
    vault_slug_dir, vault_path,        # vault (agent-generated markdown)
    repo_pdf_path, repo_paper_dir,     # repo (source material)
    repo_supplementals_dir,
    repo_upstream_dir,                 # cloned official code
    repo_sandbox_dir,                  # toy experiments
    obsidian_vault_root,               # optional: curated vault for cross-checks
)
```

For quick shell-level lookups (when the agent prefers `Shell` over Python imports):

```bash
python -m tools.paths vault <slug> <filename>   # absolute path to <vault>/<slug>/<filename>
python -m tools.paths pdf <slug>                # absolute path to <repo>/papers/<slug>/<slug>.pdf
python -m tools.paths upstream <slug>           # absolute path to <repo>/papers/<slug>/upstream/<slug>/
python -m tools.paths sandbox <slug>            # absolute path to <repo>/sandbox/<slug>/
```

If `paperlab.config.yaml` is missing, the helpers raise a clear error pointing at `paperlab.config.example.yaml`. The agent should surface that error to the user rather than guessing a path.

## Read vs. write — where each kind of file lives

| Kind | Location | Read/write |
|---|---|---|
| Paper PDF | `repo_pdf_path(slug)` | Read only |
| Supplementals | `repo_supplementals_dir(slug)` | Read only |
| Cloned upstream code | `repo_upstream_dir(slug)` | Read only (write only when cloning) |
| Toy experiments | `repo_sandbox_dir(slug)` | Read / write |
| All generated markdown (`spec.md`, `code_map.md`, `<concept>.md`, etc.) | `vault_path(slug, filename)` | Read / write |
| Slides, diagrams, tldraw | `vault_path(slug, filename)` | Read / write |
| `notes.md` | `vault_path(slug, "notes.md")` | Read (user-owned; never modify content the user wrote) |

Generated markdown NEVER lives under `<repo>/papers/`. Source material NEVER lives in the vault.

## Cross-references inside generated notes

When the `acquirer` writes `paper-info.md` (and any other note that links to repo-side material), use **absolute paths** built from `repo_pdf_path(slug)` etc. These links are machine-specific; that is acknowledged in `ROADMAP.md` under "Known limitations".

## Slug convention

**The slug is whatever the user provided. Never normalize, lowercase, hyphenate, pluralize, or otherwise alter it.** If the user said `WorldModel`, the slug is `WorldModel` — not `world-models`, not `worldmodel`, not `world-model`.

The only acceptable transformations are:
- Trim surrounding whitespace.
- If the slug literally cannot be used as a filesystem path (contains `/`, `\`, `:`, `*`, `?`, `"`, `<`, `>`, `|`, or a leading/trailing dot/space on Windows), stop and ask the user to provide an alternative. Do not silently substitute.

If the user has not provided a slug, ask for one. Do not invent it from the paper title.

## Regeneration

Before overwriting any existing file under `vault_slug_dir(slug)`, the regeneration prompt rule applies: ask **replace / append / abort**. See `.cursor/rules/paperlab-regenerate-prompt.mdc`.

## Anti-patterns

- ❌ Writing `papers/<slug>/spec.md` (old layout — no longer valid for generated markdown).
- ❌ Hard-coding the user's vault path in a skill or agent prompt.
- ❌ Building paths with f-strings outside `tools/paths.py`.
- ❌ Putting any generated `.md` under `<repo>/papers/`.
